  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nLow Confidence, Persistent Precipitation Potential After Tuesday:\n\nA relatively nebulous synoptic pattern leaves much to be desired \nregarding precipitation and storm confidence through the week. \nRegardless, a well saturated airmass south of a quasi-stationary \nboundary near or over the southern half of the local forecast area \nfrom northeast Iowa into southwest Wisconsin eventually expected to \nbe shunted along our southern periphery through the latter half of \nthe week. However, initial near term model consensus increases \nprecipitation potential due to increasing low level moisture Tuesday \nmorning and again Tuesday night. \n\nWhile overall confidence is low, the warm, moist airmass has the \npotential for enhanced instability (2000+ J/kg, LREF) due to the \nwarm frontal boundary potentially providing sufficient lift towards \nhigher deep layer shear. Most likely storm mode would quasi-linear \nor broken cellular storms as storms lift east-northeast along the \nfrontal boundary. Medium-range Real-time AI NWP Convective Hazard \nForecast models (Pangu, FengWu) paint 5% to 15% probabilities \nTuesday 12Z to Thursday 12Z as a result. \n\nConfidence decreases further through midweek as high pressure \nattempts to drive a back-door cold front across the local forecast \narea, forcing highest confidence along our southern periphery in \nnortheast Iowa. Given a sharp cutoff in precipitation and storms \nexpected, should this frontal boundary push/sag north/south, local \nPoPs will require additional adjustment.\n\nCooler Through End Of Week, Weekend Storm Chances:\n\nCanadian high pressure sagging southeast through the end of the week \nushers in a cooler, drier airmass before a passing low advecting \nprecipitation and storm chances through the weekend returns and \npersists Upper Mississippi River Valley storm/precipitation\nchances.",
